== The modules $L_zeta$ <sec:modules-l-zeta>

If
$0 != zeta in H^n (G, k) = Ext^n_(k G) (k, k)
tilde.equiv Hom_(k G) (Omega^n k, k)$
then $zeta$ corresponds to a surjective map $hat(zeta) : Omega^n k -> k$. We
write $L_zeta$#idx("$L_zeta$") for the kernel of $hat(zeta)$. If
$zeta = 0 in H^n (G, k)$, then we define $L_zeta = Omega^n k plus.o Omega k$.
The reason for this definition is as follows. If $H$ is a subgroup of $G$, then
by Schanuel's Lemma, 1.5.3 of Volume~I, we have
$Omega^n_G (k) down_H tilde.equiv Omega^n_H (k) plus.o P$ with $P$ a projective
$k H$-module. As long as $res_(G, H) (zeta)$ is still non-zero, we have
$ #l-zeta-restriction $
so that since $P$ is also injective (Proposition~3.1.2 of Volume~I) it follows
that
$ L_zeta down_H tilde.equiv L_(res_(G, H) (zeta)) plus.o ("projective"). $
If, however, $res_(G, H) (zeta) = 0$, then $hat(zeta) down_H$ has $Omega^n k$ in
its kernel and so
$ L_zeta down_H tilde.equiv Omega^n k plus.o Omega k plus.o ("projective"). $

The importance of the modules $L_zeta$ is that the variety $V_G (L_zeta)$ may be
explicitly computed.

#proposition(title: [Carlson [@bib:Carlson1984]])[
  The variety $V_G (L_zeta)$ is the hypersurface#idx("hypersurface")
  $V_G chevron.l zeta chevron.r$ in $V_G$ determined by $zeta$ (in other words,
  the set of maximal ideals containing $zeta$).
] <prop:l-zeta-variety>

#proof[
  #source(199)By Theorem~@th:module-stratification and the above discussion, it
  suffices to prove the proposition for $G = E$ elementary abelian. By
  Theorem~@th:rank-variety-equality it suffices to prove that
  $V^sharp_E (L_zeta) = V^sharp_E chevron.l zeta chevron.r$. In other words, we
  must show that if $0 != u in J(k E) slash J^2 (k E)$ and $x = 1 + u$, then
  $L_zeta down_(chevron.l x chevron.r)$ is projective if and only if
  $res_(G, chevron.l x chevron.r) (zeta) != 0$.

  If $p$ is odd and $n = deg(zeta)$ is odd, then $zeta^2 = 0$ so that
  $V_E chevron.l zeta chevron.r = V_E$, while $dim L_zeta = dim Omega^n k - 1$
  is congruent to $-2$ modulo $p$, so that $L_zeta down_(chevron.l x chevron.r)$
  is never projective. So we may assume that if $p$ is odd then $n$ is even.
  Thus $Omega^n (k) down_(chevron.l x chevron.r) tilde.equiv k plus.o P$ with
  $P$ projective. If $res_(G, chevron.l x chevron.r) (zeta) = 0$ then the map
  $hat(zeta) down_(chevron.l x chevron.r) : k plus.o P -> k$ has the first
  summand in its kernel and so
  $L_zeta down_(chevron.l x chevron.r) tilde.equiv k plus.o Omega k
  plus.o ("projective")$. If $res_(G, chevron.l x chevron.r) (zeta) != 0$ then
  the map $hat(zeta) down_(chevron.l x chevron.r) : k plus.o P -> k$ splits and
  so $L_zeta down_(chevron.l x chevron.r) tilde.equiv P$.
]

#corollary[
  Every closed homogeneous subvariety of $V_G$ is of the form $V_G (M)$ for some
  finitely generated $k G$-module $M$.
] <cor:homogeneous-subvariety-realised>

#proof[
  If $V$ is a closed homogeneous subvariety of $V_G$ then the corresponding
  ideal $I(V) subset.eq H^dot (G, k)$ is generated by homogeneous elements
  $zeta_1, dots, zeta_s$. Let
  $M = L_(zeta_1) times.o dots.c times.o L_(zeta_s)$. Then by
  Theorem~@th:variety-tensor-product and Proposition~@prop:l-zeta-variety we
  have
  $
    V_G (M) & = V_G (L_(zeta_1) times.o dots.c times.o L_(zeta_s))
              = V_G (L_(zeta_1)) inter dots.c inter V_G (L_(zeta_s)) \
            & = V_G chevron.l zeta_1 chevron.r inter dots.c inter
              V_G chevron.l zeta_s chevron.r
              = V_G chevron.l zeta_1, dots, zeta_s chevron.r = V.
  $
]

#remark[
  In order to express $V$ as
  $V_G chevron.l zeta_1 chevron.r inter dots.c inter
  V_G chevron.l zeta_s chevron.r$
  it is only necessary for some power of every element of $I(V)$ to be in the
  ideal generated by $zeta_1, dots, zeta_s$.
]

#lemma[
  If $zeta_1 in H^r (G, k)$ and $zeta_2 in H^s (G, k)$ then there is a short
  exact sequence
  $
    0 -> Omega^r L_(zeta_2) -> L_(zeta_1 zeta_2) plus.o ("projective")
    -> L_(zeta_1) -> 0.
  $
] <lem:l-zeta-product-sequence>

#proof[
  We tensor the sequence
  $0 -> L_(zeta_2) -> Omega^s k limits(->)^(hat(zeta)_2) k -> 0$ with
  $Omega^r k$ to obtain a sequence
  $
    0 -> Omega^r L_(zeta_2) plus.o ("projective")
    -> Omega^(r+s) k plus.o ("projective")
    limits(arrow.r.long)^(Omega^r (hat(zeta)_2)) Omega^r k -> 0.
  $
  #source(200)Since
  $hat(zeta_1 zeta_2) = hat(zeta)_1 compose Omega^r (hat(zeta)_2)$ (cf.
  Section~2.6 of Volume~I) we have a diagram
  $ #l-zeta-product $
  The lemma now follows from the fact that projective modules are injective
  (Proposition~3.1.2 of Volume~I).
]

The following lemma gives us another way of viewing the modules $L_zeta$.

#lemma[
  Under the isomorphism
  $
    H^n (G, k) tilde.equiv Hom_(k G) (Omega^n k, k)
    tilde.equiv Ext^1_(k G) (Omega^(n-1) k, k)
  $
  an element $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ corresponds to an extension of the form
  $ 0 -> k -> Omega^(-1) L_zeta -> Omega^(n-1) k -> 0. $
] <lem:l-zeta-extension>

#proof[
  Let $P_(n-1)$ be the $(n - 1)$st projective module in the minimal resolution
  of $k$ as a $k G$-module, so that $Omega^n k$ is a submodule of $P_(n-1)$.
  Then the lemma follows by applying the Snake Lemma, 2.3.9 of Volume~I, to the
  diagram
  $ #l-zeta-snake $
  (cf. Exercise~(v) at the end of Section~2.6 of Volume~I).
]

#proposition[
  Suppose $M$ is a finitely generated $k G$-module. Then an element
  $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ is sent to zero under the map
  $
    H^* (G, k) = Ext^*_(k G) (k, k) limits(arrow.r.long)^(times.o M)
    Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)
  $
  if and only if
  $
    Omega^(-1) L_zeta times.o M tilde.equiv M plus.o Omega^(n-1) M plus.o
    ("projective").
  $
] <prop:annihilator-criterion>

#proof[
  #source(201)By the lemma, the image of $zeta$ under the map
  $ #tensor-ext-square $
  is represented by the sequence
  $ 0 -> M -> Omega^(-1) L_zeta times.o M -> Omega^(n-1) k times.o M -> 0. $
  This image is zero if and only if this sequence splits, which by Lemma~2.6.2
  of Volume~I happens if and only if
  $
    Omega^(-1) L_zeta times.o M tilde.equiv M plus.o
    (Omega^(n-1) k times.o M).
  $
  The proposition now follows from the fact that
  $ Omega^(n-1) k times.o M tilde.equiv Omega^(n-1) M plus.o ("projective"). $
]

#remark[
  Note that $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ is sent to zero under the map
  $
    H^* (G, k) = Ext^*_(k G) (k, k) limits(arrow.r.long)^(times.o M)
    Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)
  $
  if and only if cup product with $zeta$ is identically zero on
  $Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)$. So if this happens, we say that $zeta$
  *annihilates*#idx("annihilation of cohomology")#idx(
    "cohomology",
    "annihilation of",
  ) $Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)$.
]

If $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ vanishes on $V_G (M)$, then by the definition of
$V_G (M)$, some power of $zeta$ annihilates $Ext^*_(k G) (M, M)$. In particular,
it follows from Proposition~@prop:l-zeta-variety that some power of $zeta$
annihilates $Ext^*_(k G) (L_zeta, L_zeta)$. The following proposition gives us
sharper information.

#proposition[
  If $zeta in H^n (G, k)$ then
  + $zeta^2$ annihilates $Ext^*_(k G) (L_zeta, L_zeta)$.
  + If the characteristic of the field $k$ is odd#idx(
      "characteristic",
      "odd",
    )#idx("odd characteristic") and $n$ is even then $zeta$ annihilates
    $Ext^*_(k G) (L_zeta, L_zeta)$.
] <prop:zeta-squared-annihilates>

#proof[
  + Since
    $Ext^r_(k G) (Omega^n k, L_zeta) tilde.equiv Ext^(n+r)_(k G) (k, L_zeta)$,
    the short exact sequence
    $ 0 -> L_zeta -> Omega^n k limits(->)^(hat(zeta)) k -> 0 $
    gives rise to a long exact sequence
    $
      dots.c -> Ext^r_(k G) (k, L_zeta) limits(->)^zeta
      Ext^(n+r)_(k G) (k, L_zeta) -> Ext^r_(k G) (L_zeta, L_zeta)
      -> Ext^(r+1)_(k G) (k, L_zeta) \
      limits(->)^zeta Ext^(n+r+1)_(k G) (k, L_zeta) -> dots.c
    $
    and hence to a short exact sequence
    $
      0 -> Ext^(n+r)_(k G) (k, L_zeta) slash Im(zeta)
      -> Ext^r_(k G) (L_zeta, L_zeta) \
      -> Ker(zeta "on" Ext^(r+1)_(k G) (k, L_zeta)) -> 0.
    $
    Since $zeta$ annihilates the left and right terms, $zeta^2$ annihilates the
    middle.
  + #source(202)If $bold(C)$ is a chain complex of $k G$-modules and
    $T = chevron.l sigma | sigma^2 = 1 chevron.r$ is a cyclic group of order two
    then $T$ acts on $bold(C) times.o bold(C)$ via
    $ sigma(x times.o y) = (-1)^(deg(x) deg(y)) y times.o x. $
    If the characteristic of $k$ is odd, then $bold(C) times.o bold(C)$
    decomposes into eigenspaces of $T$, which are again chain complexes of
    $k G$-modules. We define the *symmetric square*#idx(
      "square",
      "symmetric",
    )#idx("symmetric", "square") $S^2 (bold(C))$ to be the $+1$ eigenspace of
    this action, and *exterior square*#idx("exterior square")#idx(
      "square",
      "exterior",
    ) $Lambda^2 (bold(C))$ to be the $-1$ eigenspace. Taking eigenspaces on both
    sides of the Künneth formula
    $ H(bold(C) times.o bold(C)) tilde.equiv H(bold(C)) times.o H(bold(C)) $
    we obtain
    $
      H(S^2 (bold(C))) = S^2 (H(bold(C))), quad
      H(Lambda^2 (bold(C))) = Lambda^2 (H(bold(C)))
    $

    Taking the exterior square of the complex
    $ bold(C) : wide 0 -> L_zeta -> Omega^n k -> 0 $
    (whose homology is $k$ in degree zero) we obtain the complex
    $
      Lambda^2 (bold(C)) : wide 0 -> S^2 (L_zeta) -> Omega^n k times.o L_zeta
      -> Lambda^2 (Omega^n k) -> 0
    $
    which is exact since the homology of $bold(C) times.o bold(C)$ is $k$ in
    degree zero, and is hence entirely in $S^2 (bold(C))$. (Note that the
    left-hand module in $Lambda^2 (bold(C))$ is $S^2 (L_zeta)$, since $L_zeta$
    is in degree one so that $sigma$ acts via $x times.o y |-> -y times.o x$.)

    Now
    $
      Lambda^2 (Omega^n k) plus.o S^2 (Omega^n k) tilde.equiv
      Omega^(2n) k plus.o ("projective").
    $
    Since $n$ is even and $p$ is odd, $d = dim Omega^n k equiv 1 mod p$, so that
    $dim Lambda^2 (Omega^n k) = d(d - 1) slash 2 equiv 0 mod p$ and
    $dim S^2 (Omega^n k) = d(d + 1) slash 2 equiv 1 mod p$. Since projective
    modules have dimension divisible by $p$ and $Omega^(2n) k$ is
    indecomposable, the Krull–Schmidt theorem implies that we have
    $S^2 (Omega^n k) tilde.equiv Omega^(2n) k plus.o ("projective")$, and
    $Lambda^2 (Omega^n k)$ is projective. Thus the above exact sequence
    $Lambda^2 (bold(C))$ shows that
    $
      S^2 (L_zeta) plus.o ("projective") tilde.equiv Omega^n k times.o L_zeta
      tilde.equiv Omega^n (L_zeta) plus.o ("projective")
    $
    and hence
    $ S^2 (L_zeta) tilde.equiv Omega^n (L_zeta) plus.o ("projective"). $

    Similarly, if we take the symmetric square of the complex
    $ bold(C)' : wide 0 -> Omega^n k limits(->)^(hat(zeta)) k -> 0 $
    (whose homology is $L_zeta$ in degree one) we obtain a complex
    $
      S^2 (bold(C)') : wide 0 -> Lambda^2 (Omega^n k) -> Omega^n k
      limits(->)^(hat(zeta)) k -> 0
    $
    whose homology is $Lambda^2 (L_zeta)$ in degree two, and hence an exact
    sequence
    $ 0 -> Lambda^2 (L_zeta) -> Lambda^2 (Omega^n k) -> L_zeta -> 0. $
    Again using the fact that $Lambda^2 (Omega^n k)$ is projective, we see that
    $ Lambda^2 (L_zeta) tilde.equiv Omega(L_zeta) plus.o ("projective"). $
    #source(203)Putting these together, we have
    $
      L_zeta times.o L_zeta tilde.equiv Lambda^2 (L_zeta) plus.o S^2 (L_zeta)
      tilde.equiv Omega(L_zeta) plus.o Omega^n (L_zeta) plus.o ("projective")
    $
    so that
    $
      Omega^(-1) (L_zeta) times.o L_zeta tilde.equiv L_zeta plus.o
      Omega^(n-1) (L_zeta) plus.o ("projective"),
    $
    and hence by Proposition~@prop:annihilator-criterion, $zeta$ annihilates
    $Ext^*_(k G) (L_zeta, L_zeta)$.
]

#remark[
  Let $G = chevron.l x, y | x^8 = y^2 = y x y x^(-3) = 1 chevron.r$ be the
  semidihedral group#idx("group", "semidihedral")#idx("semidihedral group") of
  order 16, and $k$ be a field of characteristic two. Let $zeta$ be the element
  of $H^1 (G, k)$ corresponding to the quaternion subgroup
  $chevron.l x^2, x y chevron.r$ of order 8. Then for every $n > 1$, $zeta^n$
  does not annihilate $Ext^*_(k G) (L_(zeta^n), L_(zeta^n))$.#ed-note[A direct
    computation does not confirm this: for $n <= 5$ and each non-zero
    $zeta in H^1 (G, k)$, $zeta^n$ annihilates
    $Ext^*_(k G) (L_(zeta^n), L_(zeta^n))$. The phenomenon occurs for
    $G = ZZ slash 2 times ZZ slash 2$ and $zeta = x_1^2 + x_1 x_2 + x_2^2$.]
]

#exercise[
  [@bib:Benson1986c] Suppose that $G tilde.equiv ZZ slash p times ZZ slash p$
  with $p$ odd, and $0 != zeta in H^2 (G, FF_p)$ is in the image of the
  Bockstein map. Show that $L_zeta tilde.equiv Omega^2 (L_zeta)$.

  Show that there is a subgroup $H tilde.equiv ZZ slash p$ and a map
  $L_zeta -> FF_p$ which splits on restriction to $H$. Show that there is no
  such map $Omega(L_zeta) -> FF_p$, and therefore
  $L_zeta tilde.equiv.not Omega(L_zeta)$.

  Use Propositions~@prop:annihilator-criterion
  and~@prop:zeta-squared-annihilates to show that
  $L_zeta times.o L_zeta tilde.equiv L_zeta times.o Omega(L_zeta)$. Deduce that
  the element $[L_zeta] - [Omega(L_zeta)]$ of the representation ring $a(G)$ is
  a non-zero nilpotent element#idx(
    "$a(G)$, nilpotent elements in",
  )#idx("nilpotent elements in $a(G)$") (cf. Section~5.8 of Volume~I).
]
